<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Toamasina</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tamatave</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Toamasina, formerly Tamatave, is the main seaport of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>, on the island's east coast. In the 18th century it was a trading port of the Betsimisaraka coast, and in 1817 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radama-i">Radama I</a> of the Merina Kingdom took it, bringing the east coast under Merina rule. French forces attacked it in 1845 and 1883, and today it handles most of the country's foreign trade.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/toamasina.jpg" alt="Toamasina" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hanspeter Limacher · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tamatave_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Toamasina is the capital of the Atsinanana region, on the east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>, 215 kilometres northeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antananarivo">Antananarivo</a>. Its name means like salt or salty. In French it is called Tamatave, and in the past it was also known as Port aux prunes. Its importance comes from a coral reef that forms a spacious harbour, entered by two openings. The city centre is built on a sandy peninsula that projects at right angles from the general coastline, and the mouth of the Ivondro River lies to the south. The climate is a trade-wind tropical rainforest one, with no true dry season. September to November is the driest period and February to April the wettest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under French rule Toamasina was the seat of several foreign consuls and of many French officials, and it was the chief port for the capital and the interior. Imports were mainly piece-goods, flour-based foods and iron and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> goods, and the main exports were gold dust, raffia, hides, natural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> and live animals. Steamers of the Messageries Maritimes and the Havraise companies kept up communication with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and the British Union-Castle Line linked the port with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a> and, from there, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>.</p><p>In the colonial period the soil and a formerly crowded population left the town often plagued by epidemics. Plague broke out in 1898 and again in 1900. The draining of the neighbouring marshes brought some improvement, though malaria and other diseases have continued. The botanist and explorer André Michaux died in Tamatave in 1802.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Toamasina is the chief seaport of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> and the country's main gateway to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> and the world. The railway line to the capital ends here, and National Road 2 runs west to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antananarivo">Antananarivo</a>. The town is also the northern end of the Canal des Pangalanes. The University of Toamasina was founded in 1977. Its places of worship are mostly Christian churches, with the Roman Catholic cathedral of St Joseph, and there are also mosques.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Toamasina” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>With it he took the east-coast port of <b>Toamasina</b> in 1817, campaigned against the coastal and southern peoples, and in 1824 captured Mahajanga, the chief port of the Sakalava kingdom of Boina.</blockquote><a href="/empire/merina-kingdom">Merina Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The confederation supplied rice, cattle and slaves to French Ile Bourbon and Isle de France through <b>Toamasina</b> and Foulpointe.</blockquote><a href="/state/betsimisaraka-confederation">Betsimisaraka Confederation</a></li></ul></section>
